{"version":"2b1","blocks":[{"type":"text","text":"The dot product of two vectors is a scalar that measures how much one vector extends in the direction of another. Geometrically, it is the product of the length of the first vector and the length of the projection of the second vector onto the first."},{"type":"text","text":"If \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \) are vectors, their dot product is \( \mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b} = |\mathbf{a}| |\mathbf{b}| \cos(\theta) \), where \( \theta \) is the angle between them."},{"type":"text","text":"When the vectors are orthogonal, \( \theta = 90^\circ \), so \( \cos(90^\circ) = 0 \), and the dot product is zero. This means the vectors have no component in the direction of each other."},{"type":"text","text":"If the dot product is negative, it means the angle between the vectors is greater than 90 degrees, so the projection of one vector onto the other points in the opposite direction. This is called a negative projection."},{"type":"ui","source":"// Control to adjust angle between vectors
Control("Angle (degrees)",0,180,[],$angle)

// Convert angle to radians
$angleRad = $angle * 3.14159 / 180

// Vector a fixed along x-axis
$aX = 100
$aY = 0

// Vector b at angle
$bX = 100 * cos($angleRad)
$bY = 100 * sin($angleRad)

// Dot product
$dot = $aX * $bX + $aY * $bY

// Projection length of b onto a
$proj = $dot / $aX

// Labels
$dotLabel = "Dot Product: " + $dot
$projLabel = "Projection Length: " + $proj

// Diagram items
$vectorA = group([path("M 50 100 L 150 100 Z"), text(150, 90, "a")])
$vectorB = group([path("M 50 100 L " + (50 + $bX) + " " + (100 - $bY) + " Z"), text(50 + $bX, 100 - $bY - 10, "b")])
$projectionLine = path("M 50 100 L " + (50 + $proj) + " 100 Z")

Graphic("Vectors and Projection", [$vectorA, $vectorB, $projectionLine, text(50, 120, $dotLabel), text(50, 135, $projLabel)])","state":[{"name":"angle","value":60}]}]}

